I can’t tell from your account data alone whether you’re using the 529 well, but the main check is simple: it’s usually working well if you’re using it for qualified education expenses, not overfunding it far beyond likely education needs, and coordinating withdrawals with any education tax credits. Qualified 529 withdrawals are generally federal tax-free, and qualified uses can include college tuition, required fees, books/supplies/equipment, certain room-and-board costs for at least half-time students, up to $10,000 per year of K–12 tuition, certain apprenticeship costs, and limited student-loan repayment. (
irs.gov
)

The biggest tax caveat is no double-dipping: you generally can’t use the same education expenses both for tax-free 529 treatment and for credits like the American Opportunity Tax Credit or Lifetime Learning Credit. Tax-free scholarships and grants also reduce the pool of expenses that can support tax-free 529 withdrawals. If you withdraw more than adjusted qualified expenses, the earnings portion of the nonqualified amount can become taxable and may face a 10% additional tax. (
irs.gov
)

For flexibility, 529s are less rigid than many families think: you can usually change the beneficiary to another qualifying family member, and for distributions after 2023 there is also a path to roll some unused 529 money to the beneficiary’s Roth IRA. But that rollover has important limits: the 529 generally must have been open at least 15 years, recent contributions from the last 5 years and their earnings are excluded, the rollover is subject to the annual Roth IRA contribution limit, the beneficiary must be the Roth IRA owner, earned income is generally needed, and the lifetime rollover cap is $35,000. (
sec.gov
)

Because you’re in Colorado, there may also be a state income-tax subtraction for qualifying contributions to a CollegeInvest-administered 529 plan, subject to state rules and limits. Colorado’s published guidance and plan materials indicate the deduction is available but capped, and state treatment can differ from federal treatment for certain uses or rollovers, so that piece is worth checking carefully each tax year. (
tax.colorado.gov
)

One more practical caveat: for financial aid, FAFSA materials treat 529 plans as education savings/investment assets, and parents report a child’s 529 as part of parent investments on the FAFSA for that child. (
studentaid.gov
)

If you want, I can help assess your 529 more concretely using your child’s age, current 529 balance, annual contributions, and whether you expect in-state public, private, or graduate-school costs.
